1. Introduction
Recently, the ingestion and inhalation of Microplastic (MP) particles by humans has gotten increasing attention. MP particles have been detected in various organs in the human body (such as placentas (Ragusa et al., 2021)). Detailed studies about the harm of MP particles to the human system, however, are still missing. Three mechanism have been suggested in the literature (Gruber et al., 2022): a) MP particles can be carriers for other toxins, b) substances in the plastics can be harmful, and c) MP particles can enter human cells and cause harm, there. Mice studies showed that the gut microbiota can be considerably effected and inflammation can be induced by MP ingestion (Li et al., 2020).
It can be assumed that smaller particles are more harmful regarding mechanism c. Lim (2021) states size limits for MP particles crossing into human cells and the blood-brain barrier as 700 nm and 200 nm, respectively. Therefore, the effect of MP particles on cell cultures has been studied, such that their cytotoxicity can be evaluated. This has been done for colon cell cultures, where a distinct dependence of the toxicity mechanisms on the particle size was found (Wu et al., 2019). Another work evaluated the MP’s cytotoxicity for blood lymphocytes (Cobanoglu et al., 2021). In extreme cases of particles of a few nm, the particle size can be more relevant than the material (Akhavan et al., 2012), and other effects like surface charge can play a role for cytotoxicity (Roshanzadeh et al., 2020).
Apart from the size and type of plastic of the MP particles, the amount of ingested particles is another key factor for the toxic effects on the human body. This amount can be estimated as the total number of ingested particles ni,MP (Cox et al., 2019) or the total ingested MP mass mi,MP. Two review papers reported mi,MP and compared it to everyday objects: First, “People may eat 50 plastic bags due to MPs pollution in food every year” (Bai et al., 2022) and second, “on average we ingest five grams of MPs per week per person (roughly corresponding to the mass of a credit card)” (Gruber et al., 2022).
Bai predicts ingested MP mass from other studies that reported the ingested MP particle numbers and took a mean particle size from a review paper (Senathirajah et al., 2021). The quality of Bai’s analysis has been criticized in a letter to the editor (Lu et al., 2022), particularly for the disregard of the particle size distribution in the calculation of ingested MP mass.
Gruber refers to a WWF report (Senathirajah and Palanisami, 2019), which states that “humans may be ingesting as much as 5 g/week of microplastics”, and that “up to 5 g/week of microplastic particles is potentially ingested by humans”. The WWF report presents preliminary results of a review paper (Senathirajah et al., 2021). The statement of Gruber, albeit only a small point in their paper, has been used as the leading sentence in a press release of the Medical University of Vienna (Medical University of Vienna, 2022) and has been further spread in newspapers such as the New York Post (Kato, 2022).
The obtained mi,MP of both papers can be traced back to the calculations made by Senathirajah, who have made a first estimation of the total mass of ingested MP particles mi,MP. They combine data from other studies (MP particle size, particle count in consumables (shellfish, salt, beer, water), and amount of the yearly intake of those consumables) to calculate mi,MP. Three scenarios that differ in mean particle mass prediction yield an mi,MP of 5.5, 0.3, and 0.1 g/week, respectively.
Apart from the issue that Gruber state that mi,MP is 5 g on average and not 0.1–5 g (as criticized already in a YouTube video (Green, 2022)), the upper limit of 5 g/week seems to be unreasonably high and both the upper and lower limit lie considerably above the predictions of Mohamed Nor et al. (2021) (median value of 4.1 μg/week for adults). This work thus analyzes the mi,MP calculation method of Senathirajah and describes possible issues of their analysis. Apart from some obvious contradictions, there is also a methodological issue with the combination of particle number and particle size data from different sources.
2. Revisiting the mass mi,MP of Senathirajah
The ingested MP mass mi,MP of 0.1–5.5 g/week is calculated from the number of ingested MP particles Ni,MP times the average MP particle mass
m¯MP
 for all regarded MP sources (shellfish, salt, beer, and drinking water). Ni,MP is calculated as the number of particles nMP per kg (shellfish, salt) or per liter (beer, drinking water) times the ingested amount Ai of this source:
(1)
mi,MP=∑cons.Ni,MPm¯MP=∑cons.nMPAim¯MP
The mean particle mass and the particle count are obtained from different studies. The amount of ingested consumables follows other studies or dietary recommendations. Senathirajah assumes that humans consume 219 liters of bottled water and 3.25–4.38 kg of salt each year.
2.1. Mean particle mass
The mean MP mass
m¯MP
 is taken from reported MP particle counts and their corresponding total mass (scenario 1) or from particle size distributions (scenarios 2 and 3). In scenario 1 of Senathirajah, drinking water accounts for 90% of mi,MP. In scenarios 2 and 3, salt accounts for 96% of mi,MP, so the calculation of
m¯MP
 for water and salt are discussed in the following.
2.1.1. Scenario 1: mMP in water
Scenario 1 obtains the mean particle mass from studies that report the total mass of detected MP particles MMP and the corresponding number of MP particles NMP. For the ingested MP particles in drinking water, this is done using data from the oceanic environment. Senathirajah regards works with minimum and maximum mass and particle count (It is not clear how they distinguish between the two) and Senathirajah lists them in the supplementary materials in Table S5 and S6. Figure 1 plots these data points of NMP and MMP. Note that with a double-logarithmic scale, points on a line with the slope 1 share the same mMP. Most points originate from one study (Eriksen et al., 2014) and are plotted using x-markers. Eriksen reports different size ranges in different oceans, and Senathirajah uses the size range of 0.33 mm – 1 mm to obtain the mean particle mass. Some data points in the supplementary materials of Senathirajah were supposedly from the study of Eriksen, but cannot be found, there (these data points are marked as black x-signs in Figure 1). The references of the other data points can be found in table S2 of Senathirajah.
