3. Issues with predicted mi,MP of Senathirajah
As mentioned in Section 2, the mi,MP calculation of Senathirajah is not comprehensive and some of their calculated
m¯MP
 can be considered as flawed. Furthermore, the MP mass for drinking water is estimated from MP measurements in the oceans and the particle counts per liter from drinking water, where the particle sizes could be quite different. For example, distinct differences in the particle size distributions in different environments has been reported (Kooi, 2021). Furthermore, the calculated
m¯MP
 for water in scenario 1 and salt in scenarios 2 and 3 of Senathirajah do not make sense:
•
For
m¯MP
 in water, scenario 1 is supposed to use studies with particle sizes between 0 mm and 1 mm to estimate the MP mass. However, for their mean particle mass of 2.8 mg and the density of 1.4 g/cm3 they assume for MPs in water, their size is bigger than 1 mm: A diameter of 1.56 mm for a spherical particle and an edge length of 1.26 mm for a cubic particle. This issue of has already been pointed out (Kim and Song, 2021), and is already evident in the study (Eriksen et al., 2014) that Senathirajah base their calculation on.
•
The obtained mean MP particle size of 2.94 mm in salt of scenarios 2 and 3 does not seem plausible. It is based on two studies. The first study (Karami et al., 2017) reports
amin=149
 μm,
amax=1
 mm, and
a¯MP=0.515
 mm, with most of the MP particles being fragment-shaped. The second study (Kim et al., 2018) detects particles with aMP between 0.1 mm and 5 mm. In the supplementary materials, Kim take into account the detected MP shapes to compute an MP content in salt of 0.1–100 mg MP/kg salt. When assuming an annual salt consumption of 3.25–4.38 kg from Senathirajah, this results in a yearly MP consumption from salt between 0.00033 g and 0.44 g and not 7.4 g and 14.2 g, as Senathirajah obtains in scenario 2 and 3, respectively.
Even for realistic mean particle sizes
m¯MP
, there remains the issue of combining
m¯MP
 and nMP from measurements with different detection limits
amin
. Senathirajah uses a much smaller
amin
 for nMP than for
m¯MP
. The cited works for nMP state that most of the detected particles were in the smallest size range of a few μm. It is thus obvious that assuming bigger MP particles with a size above 1 mm and then using MP counts, where particles down to 1 μm were detected, leads to a vast overestimation of mi,MP.
Even in scenarios 2 and 3, where mi,MP is 0.1 g/week and 0.3 g/week, respectively, the assumption of the spherical or cubic particle shape is quite questionable and does not match the observations from literature. For example, in the particle size range above 50 μm (which contributes most to the total MP mass), fragments and fibers make up most of the detected MP particles (Lim, 2021). Considering the particle size as the biggest dimension (Kim et al., 2018), fragments and fibers have considerably lower masses than spheres or cubes for the same particle size.
4. Computing mi,MP using particle size distributions
In scenarios 2 and 3, Senathirajah uses one study (Cai et al., 2018) that report a particle size distribution in drinking water. Figure 3 shows this distribution and a similar distribution from literature (Erni-Cassola et al., 2017) as bar plots. Cai and Erni in total detected 200 particles with
amin=50
 μm and 199 particles with
amin=20
 μm, respectively. Erni argues that this size distribution can be approximated by a power-law function, written as
dnda=g(a)=Ba−r
, with g(a) denoting the density function of the particle number, a the particle size, and B and r fitting parameters. The function g(a) is defined such that its integral yields the number of particles in the range (
amin,amax
):
(2)
nMP=∫aminamaxg(a)da=B1−ramax1−r−amin1−r
