Fig. 3. Bar plot of reported particle counts depending on the particle size aMP (Cai et al., 2018, Erni-Cassola et al., 2017. The bar height corresponds to the particles divided by the a range of the bar. The dashed curves have been fitted to the bars.

Down to a certain particle size of about 1 μm, this function approximates the PSD of MP particles well (Kooi, 2021). It is widely used to approximate measured PSDs in aquatic environments (Erni-Cassola et al., 2017, Buonassissi and Dierssen, 2010, Kooi, 2021, Runyan et al., 2020. Least square fits of the particle size distributions are plotted as dashed lines in Figure 3. Note that the for the fit of the Cai data, the leftmost bar was ignored, because their detection limit was 50 μm so that only few particles below 50 μm were detected. The exponent r increases from 1.67 to 1.98 with decreasing
amin
, indicating that the actual r might be higher.
This fits with r values reported in other works that deal with MP particle size distributions: MP particles were analyzed in oceans and r values between 2.7 and 4.7 were obtained (Buonassissi and Dierssen, 2010). Another study (Kooi, 2021) reports r = 2.2 at the marine surface, 3.1 in biotas, and states that the obtained r depends on the environment and polymer type. Furthermore, an r = 2.87 – 4 was obtained in arctic oceans (Runyan et al., 2020).
In the following, the Particle Size Distribution (PSD) is used to evaluate errors due to the disregard of the particle size distribution in predicting mi,MP. With the volume of one particle V depending on the particle size a, the PSD function can predict the total MP mass MMP in the size range (
amin,amax
) as
(3)
MMP=∫aminamaxg(a)V(a)da.
The volume V(a) is a linear, quadratic, or cubic function of a for the 1d (fiber), 2d (flake), and 3d (spheres, cubes) particles, respectively: V = C ad, with C as a constant and d denoting the number of dimensions. The mass then calculates as
(4)
MMP=CBρr−d−1amax1+d−r−amin1+d−r.
The total MP mass can thus be calculated based on the PSD function parameters. To avoid issues with unrealistically big particles or unrealistic particle counts for a approaching zero, in the following, mi,MP is obtained in the size range of 1 μm to 1 mm.
In contrast to Senathirajah, Mohamed Nor et al. (2021) use the above-stated power-law PSD function in their analysis. They take the particle size distributions from other studies, exclude data below their detection limit, and fit the two parameters of the power law. Thus, they calculate the amount of particles with a size between 1 μm and 5 mm. They furthermore consider distributions in the density and aspect ratio of the particles to increase the accuracy of the computed mi,MP results. The obtained mi,MP of Mohamed Nor et al. (2021) can be considered to be as accurate as it gets regarding the limitations of data available in the literature (for 8 food sources and air, Mohamed Nor et al. (2021) used 12 studies with 15 data-sets and assumed similar particle size distributions for all food sources).
