5. Conclusions
Senathirajah combines data of studies that differ in detected particle size and source to obtain the total ingested MP mass mi,MP of 0.1–5 g/week. Considering the size distribution of MP particles, which approximately follows a power-law function, this combination can induce mi,MP errors by several orders of magnitude. Furthermore, the evaluation of mean particle mass for water in scenario 1 contradicts their assumptions: It is stated that a particle size range of 0–1 mm is investigated, but the mean particle mass of 2.8 mg corresponds to particle sizes above 1 mm. Also, the salt particle size of 2.94 mm is not reproducible and unrealistically high.
Assuming spherical particles and an annual consumption stated by Senathirajah (219 liters of bottled water and an average of 3.8 kg of salt per year), one liter of water on average contains 420 MP particles with a diameter of 1.56 mm and one kg of salt contains 148 MP particles with a diameter of 2.94 mm.
In combination with the particle counts that detected particles down to about 5 μm and the unrealistic assumption of spherical or cubic particle shapes, it is obvious that both the maximum and minimum mi,MP calculated by Senathirajah considerably overestimate the MP ingestion. More comprehensible data on mi,MP is reported in a recent study (Mohamed Nor et al., 2021) that evaluates the ingested MP mass with the aim to predict the accumulation of MP in human bodies. Mohamed Nor does account for the size ranges of the used studies and also for the particle shapes, and obtains a median mi,MP of 4.1 μg/week for adults. They cite Senathirajah and the WWF report, and dryly state that their mi,MP of up to 5 g/week “does not represent the intake of an average person”. Indeed, the mi,MP of 5 g/week is by a factor of about 106 higher than their median value, which means that a human eats a credit card worth of MPs not every week but every 23 thousand years.
Furthermore, mi,MP is not a relevant parameter for assessing the harm to humans. It is widely agreed upon that smaller particles can enter the bloodstream and organs more easily than bigger particles. For very small sizes of about 10 nm, the material of the particles might not even play a role any more (Akhavan et al., 2012). However, detailed studies of MP particles on the human body are still missing. Therefore, future work should identify the mechanisms of toxic effects of MP particles depending on the particle size. The size ranges with the highest toxicity of MP particles could be specifically detected in future studies and concepts for reducing the number of MP particles in these size ranges can be proposed.
